section.para:
The endpoint /stib/vehicle-trip returns the latest positions as GeoJSON. The table live_vehicles keeps the trajectory of each run, and the function stib_ingest appends the positions of a snapshot to it with the function appendInstant. A snapshot read twice appends nothing, since a position is only appended after the last one of its trajectory.
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS live_vehicles; CREATE TABLE live_vehicles ( vehicle_id text PRIMARY KEY, line_id text, route_id text, trip_id ttext, trip tgeompoint );
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION stib_ingest(snapshot jsonb) RETURNS integer AS $$ WITH f AS ( SELECT f->'properties' AS p, ST_Transform(ST_SetSRID(ST_GeomFromGeoJSON(f->'geometry'), 4326), 31370) AS geom FROM jsonb_array_elements(snapshot->'features') AS f ), n AS ( SELECT p->>'uuid' AS vehicle_id, p->>'lineId' AS line_id, p->>'routeId' AS route_id, to_timestamp((p->>'timestamp')::float) AS t, p->>'tripId' AS trip_id, geom FROM f ), upsert AS ( INSERT INTO live_vehicles AS l (vehicle_id, line_id, route_id, trip_id, trip) SELECT vehicle_id, line_id, route_id, ttext(trip_id, t), tgeompoint(geom, t) FROM n ON CONFLICT (vehicle_id) DO UPDATE SET trip = appendInstant(l.trip, EXCLUDED.trip), trip_id = CASE WHEN EXCLUDED.trip_id IS NULL THEN l.trip_id WHEN l.trip_id IS NULL THEN EXCLUDED.trip_id ELSE appendInstant(l.trip_id, EXCLUDED.trip_id) END WHERE getTimestamp(EXCLUDED.trip) > endTimestamp(l.trip) RETURNING 1 ) SELECT count(*)::integer FROM upsert; $$ LANGUAGE sql;

The following script polls the endpoint every 20 seconds and passes each snapshot to the function. The command \set of psql reads the snapshot into a variable, which a command-line argument could not hold.
#!/bin/bash # Poll the STIB vehicle positions of MobilityTwin.Brussels every 20 seconds # and append them to the trajectories of table live_vehicles. # Usage: MOBILITYTWIN_TOKEN=... ./stib_live.sh [database] DB=${1:-stib} URL=https://api.mobilitytwin.brussels/stib/vehicle-trip while true; do if curl -sf -H "Authorization: Bearer $MOBILITYTWIN_TOKEN" "$URL" -o snapshot.json; then psql -X -q -At -d "$DB" <<'EOF' \set s `cat snapshot.json` SELECT now()::time(0) AS polled, stib_ingest(:'s') AS appended; EOF fi sleep 20 done Each line printed is a poll and the number of runs whose trajectory grew. While the script runs, the vehicles that have been standing still for at least 3 minutes away from any stop are obtained as follows.
SELECT l.line_id, l.vehicle_id, duration(s)::interval(0) AS stopped_for, ST_AsText(ST_Transform(endValue(s), 4326)) AS position FROM live_vehicles l, unnest(sequences(stops(atTime(l.trip, span(now() - interval '15 minutes', now())), 20.0, interval '3 minutes'))) AS s WHERE tempSubType(l.trip) <> 'Instant' AND startTimestamp(s) > startTimestamp(l.trip) AND endTimestamp(s) = endTimestamp(l.trip) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM stops p WHERE ST_DWithin(p.stop_geom, endValue(s), 50.0)); The function atTime keeps the last 15 minutes of each trajectory, and a vehicle is still standing when its last stop ends with its trajectory. As for the day of service, a stop starting with the trajectory is left out: the vehicle has not moved since the script started, which is how a layover looks.
